  "description": "Compute multi-head self-attention on patch token representations with QKV projection and output projection.\n\nUnlike localized 2D convolutional receptive fields, multi-head self-attention models pairwise relationships across all visual patches immediately from the first transformer layer.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{Y} = \\text{Concat}(\\text{head}_1, \\dots, \\text{head}_H) \\mathbf{W}_{\\text{out}}, \\quad \\text{head}_h = \\text{Softmax}\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{Q}_h \\mathbf{K}_h^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) \\mathbf{V}_h\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Project input sequence x through fused QKV matrix to obtain query, key, and value tensors\n2. Partition projections into multiple attention heads of dimension d_k = D / H\n3. Compute scaled dot-product attention per head, concatenate context representations, and project via W_out\n\n### Returns\nNested 2D list of shape (N, D) representing projected multi-head attention output rounded to 4 decimal places.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: ViT Multi-Head Self-Attention\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nImages have unique spatial properties: neighboring pixels are strongly correlated, and visual patterns (like edges, corners, or textures) appear anywhere in an image. Computer vision algorithms use spatial inductive biases like translation equivariance and local receptive fields to process image grids efficiently. **ViT Multi-Head Self-Attention** operates directly on spatial coordinates or bounding box geometries.\n\nUnlike localized 2D convolutional receptive fields, multi-head self-attention models pairwise relationships across all visual patches immediately from the first transformer layer. In this problem, the goal is to: compute multi-head self-attention on patch token representations with qkv projection and output projection.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{Y} = \\text{Concat}(\\text{head}_1, \\dots, \\text{head}_H) \\mathbf{W}_{\\text{out}}, \\quad \\text{head}_h = \\text{Softmax}\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{Q}_h \\mathbf{K}_h^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) \\mathbf{V}_h\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Project input sequence x through fused QKV matrix to obtain query, key, and value tensors\n2. **Phase 2**: Partition projections into multiple attention heads of dimension d_k = D / H\n3. **Phase 3**: Compute scaled dot-product attention per head, concatenate context representations, and project via W_out\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Spatial Locality**: Sliding spatial filters across multi-channel feature maps aggregates contextual clues across local neighborhoods.\n* **Object Detection Pipelines**: Bounding box metrics (such as IoU and Non-Maximum Suppression) reconcile duplicate candidate detections into clean, final predictions.\n* **Data Augmentation**: Geometric transformations expose models to diverse spatial viewpoints, significantly improving generalization to unseen test images.",
